for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    sum += i;
    System.out.println("i = " + i + ", sum = " + sum);
}
```

The **for** loop keeps initialisation, test and update in one header, separated by semicolons. The test is made **before** every pass.

```java
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    sum += i;
    System.out.println("i = " + i + ", sum = " + sum);
}
```

Trace the loop: for each value of `i`, check the test, run the body and note `sum`.

```java
int i = 3;
while (i <= 15) {
    System.out.print(i + " ");
    i += 3;
}
```

The **while** loop has only the test in its header. The initialisation goes **before** the loop and the update **inside** the body.

```java
int i = 3;
do {
    System.out.print(i + " ");
    i += 3;
} while (i <= 15);
```

In **do-while** the body comes first and the test is made **after** it. The condition is followed by a **semicolon**.

```java
for (int i = 3; i <= 15; i += 3)
    System.out.print(i + " ");
```

Rewrite this loop as a **while** loop and as a **do-while** loop. Move the initialisation before the loop, put the update at the end of the body, and keep the same test.

```java
for (int a = 1, b = 10; a < b; a += 2, b -= 2)
    System.out.print(a + "-" + b + " ");
```

Several variables can be initialised and updated in one header, separated by **commas**. The test is a single condition.

A loop whose condition **never becomes false** is an **infinite loop**. It is shown here but never run.

```java
while (true) {
    System.out.println("Hello");
}
```

A bounded form that ends with **break** prints `4`.

```java
