for (int w = 1; w <= 1000000; w++);
System.out.println("Ready");
```

A **delay loop** has an **empty body**, written as a semicolon directly after the header. It only counts, and so uses up time. The same semicolon placed by mistake after a loop header gives surprising results.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    if (i == 5)
        break;
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}
System.out.println("done");
```

The **break** statement leaves the loop at once. Control goes to the first statement after the loop.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    if (i == 5)
        continue;
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}
System.out.println("done");
```

The **continue** statement skips the rest of the **current pass**. In a for loop control goes to the update, and then to the test.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 12; i++) {
    if (i % 5 == 0)
        continue;
    if (i * i > 60)
        break;
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}
```

Trace the loop for each `i`: first the test for `continue`, then the test for `break`, then the print.

```java
int n = 4825;
while (n > 0) {
    int d = n % 10;
    System.out.println(d);
    n = n / 10;
}
```

For an int `n`, **n % 10** is the last digit and **n / 10** removes it. Repeat while `n > 0`.

```java
int n = 4825, count = 0, sum = 0;
while (n > 0) {
    int d = n % 10;
    count++;
    sum += d;
    n /= 10;
}
System.out.println("Digits = " + count);
System.out.println("Sum = " + sum);
```

Trace the loop for 4825: take the digit, count it, add it, and remove it.

```java
int n = 3472, rev = 0;
while (n > 0) {
    int d = n % 10;
    rev = rev * 10 + d;
    n = n / 10;
}
System.out.println(rev);
```

Each digit is added at the right end of `rev`: **rev = rev * 10 + d**.

```java
int n = 1221, t = n, rev = 0;
while (t > 0) {
    rev = rev * 10 + t % 10;
    t /= 10;
}
if (rev == n)
    System.out.println(n + " is a palindrome");
else
    System.out.println(n + " is not a palindrome");
```

A number is a **palindrome** if it equals its **reverse**. Work on a **copy** of the number so that the original is still available for the comparison.

```java
int n = 29, f = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (n % i == 0)
        f++;
}
if (f == 2)
    System.out.println(n + " is prime");
else
    System.out.println(n + " is not prime");
```

A **prime number** has exactly **two factors**, 1 and itself. Count the factors from 1 to n and test whether the count is 2.

```java
int n = 28, s = 0;
for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    if (n % i == 0)
        s += i;
}
if (s == n)
    System.out.println(n + " is perfect");
else
    System.out.println(n + " is not perfect, factors add up to " + s);
```

A **perfect number** equals the **sum of its factors other than itself**. The loop therefore stops at **n - 1**.

```java
int n = 153, t = n, digits = 0, sum = 0;
while (t > 0) {
    digits++;
    t /= 10;
}
t = n;
while (t > 0) {
    int d = t % 10;
    sum += (int) Math.pow(d, digits);
    t /= 10;
}
if (sum == n)
    System.out.println(n + " is an Armstrong number");
else
    System.out.println(n + " is not an Armstrong number, sum = " + sum);
```

An **Armstrong number** equals the sum of its digits, each raised to the **power of the number of digits**. Count the digits first, then add the powers with `(int) Math.pow(d, digits)`.

```java
int a = 0, b = 1;
System.out.print(a + " " + b);
for (int i = 3; i <= 8; i++) {
    int c = a + b;
    System.out.print(" " + c);
    a = b;
    b = c;
}
```

Each new term is the **sum of the two terms before it**. The new term is found first, and then a and b move one term forward.

```java
int a = 84, b = 120, hcf = 1;
for (int i = 1; i <= a && i <= b; i++) {
    if (a % i == 0 && b % i == 0)
        hcf = i;
}
int lcm = a * b / hcf;
System.out.println("HCF = " + hcf);
System.out.println("LCM = " + lcm);
```

The loop remembers the **last** common factor, which is the largest: the **HCF**. Then **LCM = a * b / hcf**.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= 5; j++)
        System.out.print("*");
    System.out.println();
}
```

The outer loop counts the **rows**, the inner loop prints the items of **one row**, and `System.out.println()` after the inner loop starts a **new line**.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++)
        System.out.print(j);
    System.out.println();
}
```

In a **triangle** the inner limit **depends on the outer variable**, for example `j <= i`. Other limits such as `5 - i` make the rows shorter instead.

```java
for (int i = 5; i >= 1; i--) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++)
        System.out.print("*");
    System.out.println();
}
```

Here the **outer** variable counts **down** from 5 to 1, and the inner loop prints `i` stars.

```java
double x = 3, s = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    s += Math.pow(x, i - 1) / i;
    System.out.println("term " + i + ", sum = " + s);
}
```

The series `1 + x/2 + x^2/3 + ...` has the term `Math.pow(x, i - 1) / i`. Add each term to a **sum that starts at 0**.

sections.body_md:
The **while** loop has the test in its header; the initialisation comes before the loop and the update is inside the body. The **do-while** loop runs the body first and tests afterwards, so it runs **at least once**; its condition ends with a semicolon.

```java
int i = 3;
while (i <= 15) {
    System.out.print(i + " ");
    i += 3;
}
```

```java
int i = 3;
do {
    System.out.print(i + " ");
    i += 3;
} while (i <= 15);
```

Both print `3 6 9 12 15`, as does the for loop `for (int i = 3; i <= 15; i += 3)`.

With n = 10 and the condition n < 5 the while loop prints `after the loop, n = 10` only, but the do-while loop prints `body runs, n = 10 / after the loop, n = 11`.

**Counting passes:** list the values of the loop variable that satisfy the test. For example `for (int i = 2; i <= 20; i += 4)` takes the values 2 6 10 14 18, so the body runs 5 times.

**break** ends the loop at once; control goes to the first statement after the loop. **continue** skips the rest of the current pass; in a for loop control goes to the update and then to the test. Both are written inside an if in the body, and neither may be used outside a loop (or switch).

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    if (i == 5)
        break;
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}
System.out.println("done");
```

Output: `1 2 3 4 done`. With continue in place of break the output is `1 2 3 4 6 7 8 done`.

**Digits:** for an int n, `n % 10` is the last digit and `n / 10` removes it; loop while n > 0. Digit sum of 4825 and number of digits: `Digits = 4 / Sum = 19`.

**Reverse and palindrome:** `rev = rev * 10 + d` (the reverse of 3472 is 2743); a number is a palindrome if it equals its reverse; work on a copy.

**Prime:** exactly two factors. `29 is prime`; `21 is not prime`. With a boolean flag and break the search stops at the first divisor: `91 is not prime`.

**Perfect:** equals the sum of its factors other than itself (loop to n - 1): `28 is perfect`; `12 is not perfect, factors add up to 16`.

**Armstrong:** equals the sum of its digits each raised to the power of the number of digits, using `(int) Math.pow(d, digits)`: `153 is an Armstrong number`; `123 is not an Armstrong number, sum = 36`.

**Fibonacci:** each term is the sum of the two before it; compute c first, then a = b and b = c:

```java
int a = 0, b = 1;
System.out.print(a + " " + b);
for (int i = 3; i <= 8; i++) {
    int c = a + b;
    System.out.print(" " + c);
    a = b;
    b = c;
}
```

Output: `0 1 1 2 3 5 8 13`.

**HCF and LCM:** the HCF is the last i that divides both numbers, and the LCM is `a * b / hcf`: for 84 and 120, `HCF = 12 / LCM = 840`. Euclid's way (repeat the remainder) also gives 12 for 84 and 36.

A **nested loop** has one loop in the body of another. The inner loop runs completely for each pass of the outer loop, so its body executes (outer passes) x (inner passes) times. In pattern programs the **outer loop counts the rows**, the **inner loop prints one row**, and `System.out.println();` after the inner loop starts a new line.

**Rectangle** (inner limit fixed):

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= 5; j++)
        System.out.print("*");
    System.out.println();
}
```

Output: `***** / ***** / *****` (lines shown separated by a slash).

**Right-angled triangle** (inner limit depends on i):

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++)
        System.out.print(j);
    System.out.println();
}
```

Output: `1 / 12 / 123 / 1234`. The limit `5 - i` makes the rows shorter, and counting the outer variable down also reverses the shape.

To find the sum of a **series**, write the term in terms of the loop variable i and add it to a sum that starts at 0. Use `Math.pow(x, i)` for powers (it returns a double) and `1.0 / i` for fractions, because `1 / i` with two ints is an integer division.

```java
double x = 3, s = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    s += Math.pow(x, i - 1) / i;
    System.out.println("term " + i + ", sum = " + s);
}
```

The last line shows the sum of 1 + x/2 + x^2/3 + x^3/4 for x = 3: `term 4, sum = 12.25`.

