answer.model_answer_md:
**break** terminates the loop and passes control to the statement after the loop. **continue** skips the remaining statements of the current pass and passes control to the next pass (in a for loop, to the update expression).

For the same loop that tests i == 5, break prints `1 2 3 4 done`, but continue prints `1 2 3 4 6 7 8 done`.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    if (i == 5)
        break;
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}
System.out.println("done");
```

An **infinite loop** is a loop whose condition never becomes false, so it keeps repeating and never ends by itself. Examples are a loop with the condition true or a loop whose update statement is missing:

```java
while (true) {
    System.out.println("Hello");
}
```

```java
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

It can be stopped by a **break** inside an if statement, or by correcting the update so that the condition becomes false. The bounded form `while (true) { count++; if (count == 4) break; }` stops and prints `4`.

```java
System.out.println("Start");
for (int w = 1; w <= 1000000; w++);
System.out.println("Ready");
```

Output: `Start / Ready`. The for loop has an **empty body** (the semicolon after the header), so it only counts from 1 to 1000000 and in doing so uses up time. A larger limit gives a longer delay.

